In news:<1134729349.336190.254460@g44g2000cwa.googlegroups.com>, L <L_1
_L_0@yahoo.com> says...
> The best (and, for the purposes of this post, accepted) theory of Eye
> of the Aethiopica's nomenclature is that "the Aethiopica" refers to
> _Threskiornis aethiopicus_, also known as the Sacred Ibis. Thus the
> reasoning behind its name is:
> Aethiopica (scientific name of...) -> Ibis (animal form of...) -> Thoth
> (god of magic).

Errmh. Aethiopica just means 'of Ethiopia', and I haven't thought it 
obvious that it would point to the theskiornis/ibis birds. Even though I 
might agree that the 'Toth - Sacred Ibis' link is something that could 
be seen adding some validity to this, I think that you will see that it 
is also linked to the other wizard deities in Nethack, among my 
ramblings below.

Some combination usages of 'Aethiopica' to indicate that something is 
'of Ethiopia':
* dysaethesia aethiopica - (warning, racism ahead!) name for a 
psychiatric disorder that a US physician 150 years ago was using to 
describe the lack of motivation among 'negro' slaves (well, I hope we've 
made some progress since...)
* paranthropus/(par)australopithecus aethiopicus - extinct species, 
remains of which have been found in Ethiopia
* protopterus aethiopicus - marbled lungfish, found in Ethiopia
* xylopia aethiopica - evergreen tree, found in Ethiopia
* zantedeschia aethiopica - giant white arum lily, found in Ethiopia
...et cetera

Let's look at the idea that the name should just be read as 'The Eye of 
Ethiopia'... All the wizard deities in Nethack are from the Egyptian 
pantheon (hmm, a bit blasphemous to use 'pantheon' about non-greco-roman 
gods? but that seems the way of the English language in this case.).
Now I'm not the expert on ancient Egypt, but AFAIK in ancient Egypt the 
southern parts of Egypt *OR* the lands south of Egypt (someone probably 
know this better) was referred to as Ethiopia. Much later the name 
traveled to be used for the region of today's Ethiopia.
The ancient Greeks also knew of Ethiopia (still not the modern one, same 
region as the ancient Egyptians), and considered the Ethiopians a sacred 
people, especially loved by the gods.
Did perhaps the Egyptians also have this view of the Ethiopians? Perhaps 
the 'Aethiopica' could be read as indicating that it simply 'has 
something to do with the gods'?

But, furthermore ... If the name was just intended to be read as 'The 
Eye of Ethiopia' it would better be formulated as 'The Eye Aethiopica". 
The current naming says that it is 'The Eye of the "of Ethiopia"' (sic!) 
- hmmm... This seems to indicate that the eye is not directly related to 
Ethiopia, but instead related to one of these things that are 'of 
Ethiopia'.

Hmm...

Like the sacred ibis. But I still don't feel very comfortable with this 
explanation, too much is left out. I'm more inclined to agree that it 
refers to the 'Aethopica' novel by Heliodorus of Emesa that Dylan 
suggests. This novel features a main character Charicleia, who has a 
dream about an eye being cut out, and is later thought to be under the 
influence of an 'evil eye'. The tale takes place in ancient 
Egypt/Ethiopia/Greece and features some magic and Egyptian gods (Isis, 
at least).

